# 8. Outlier Removal

The authors investigate two approaches for detecting and removing outliers:

## 8.1 Interquartile Range (IQR)

The IQR method uses:

```text
IQR = Q3 - Q1
```

where:

* Q1 = first quartile
* Q3 = third quartile

It is useful for identifying values that lie outside the expected distribution.

---

## 8.2 Standard Deviation Method

The authors also investigate a method based on three standard deviations.

Values beyond approximately:

```text
Mean ± 3 × Standard Deviation
```

are treated as outliers.

Different scenarios use either IQR-based or standard-deviation-based outlier removal.

---

# 9. Data Scaling

The paper investigates two major scaling techniques.

## 9.1 Standardization

Standardization transforms the feature distribution so that it approximately has:

* mean = 0
* standard deviation = 1

This is especially useful when different features have very different numerical ranges.

---

## 9.2 Normalization

Normalization maps feature values into a bounded range using the minimum and maximum values.

Conceptually:

```text
Normalized Value =
(Value - Minimum) / (Maximum - Minimum)
```

The paper evaluates both standardization and normalization in different scenarios.

---

# 10. SMOTE

One of the important techniques used in the paper is:

**Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE)**.

The original dataset is imbalanced because the Normal class contains most of the observations.

SMOTE creates synthetic samples for minority classes.

Instead of simply duplicating existing minority samples, SMOTE generates new synthetic samples by interpolating between existing minority-class observations.

The purpose is to create a more balanced training dataset.

The paper uses SMOTE as a major data augmentation strategy.

---

# 11. Train-Test Split

The dataset is divided into:

* **75% training data**
* **25% test data**

Approximately 1,600 samples are used for training.

The remaining approximately 25% are used as an independent test set.

The paper states that the test set is not used during model training.

This is important because the model should be evaluated on data it has not seen during training.

